  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper tests scalability in industrial recommendation, a new setting, and reports performance against model scale and computational complexity. It does not test language-model pretraining loss or the original parameter-token-compute setup.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports a power-law relationship between MTGR performance and computational complexity, along with offline and online performance for three model sizes. It does not report held-out language-model cross-entropy, small-run extrapolation, or a compute-optimal parameter/token allocation. The ablations in Table 4 compare model components, not the scaling relationship. Results from cited studies are not recorded.",
    "test_reason": "The paper scales its own MTGR ranking model over compute on Meituan recommendation data. It reports offline AUC/GAUC for three sizes (5.47–55.76 GFLOPs/example), online A/B deltas, and a figure it describes as a power law between CTCVR GAUC gain and compute. This is a new-domain result on V5. It is weak: the measure is ranking AUC/GAUC, not held-out loss; there is no fitted exponent; and the main table has only three sizes spanning about 10x compute. The learning rate also changes with size.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors claim a power-law relation between MTGR performance and computational complexity. They also claim scalable offline and online gains over DLRM while retaining cross features. They add that removing cross features erases the large model's gain, which scaling cannot compensate for.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is an industrial recommendation-ranking study using MTGR/HSTU and ranking/online metrics, not a language-model held-out cross-entropy sweep over parameters, tokens, and compute.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports separate sweeps over HSTU block count, model dimension, and training sequence length; it does not report fitted exponents or fitting-method sensitivity.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM '25)",
    "problems": "The scaling sweep's plotted values are not numerically reported in the text. No uncertainty estimates are reported. The evidence is specific to one industrial recommendation setting and does not test the language-model loss or compute-optimal allocation claims.",
    "rows_left_out": "Table 4 component ablations were left out because they compare cross features, GLN, and dynamic masking rather than the scaling relationship. DLRM comparisons not expressed as the reported scaling sweep or size-specific MTGR performance were also left out.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Power-law form in held-out loss (only AUC/GAUC and online CTR metrics are reported). No fitted exponents. No data-size or token scaling with numbers. No image/video, repeated-data, fine-tuning or transfer regimes."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [
      {
        "what": "Labelled CTCVR GAUC values in the Fig. 3(a–c) sweeps (e.g. blocks 2–8: 0.6543–0.6615; seq len 100–5000: 0.6551–0.6625) are readable but recorded as figure-only.",
        "locator": "Figure 3(a)-(c)",
        "versions": "V5"
      },
      {
        "what": "Text claim that MTGR's gains over DLRM grow as training tokens increase. It is stated in words only, with no numbers.",
        "locator": "Section 6.5",
        "versions": "V5"
      }
    ],
    "verdict_notes": "Every scaling point is the authors' own method with a size-specific learning rate. There are no uncertainty estimates, and the 'power-law' is asserted from a plot without a fit. The compute span in Table 3 is about 10x; Fig. 3(d) spans roughly 2^-2 to 2^6 relative to UserTower-SIM. The comparisons against DLRM baselines are method comparisons, not scaling evidence. Only the within-MTGR trend across sizes bears on V5."
  },
